---
/**
 * SiteHeader — the global shell for the redesign (implementation brief §4).
 *
 * FOUR flat doors, no dropdowns, no mega-menu.
 *
 * THE CASE IS NO LONGER A PEER TOP-LEVEL DOOR (Huayin, 2026-08-25). It remains public as the
 * evidence record and is reached through Columna, which already carried six editorial links to it
 * before this change — by far the strongest inbound path on the site. Removing a door from an
 * otherwise unprotected route is the real risk here, so the same change adds /case to
 * scripts/check_discoverability.mjs: the ruling becomes a gate rather than a convention. `Analytical Governance` is never shortened to
 * `Governance`: in this market bare "governance" already means stewardship/access/compliance, and
 * the whole point of the category is that it concerns the validity and authority of analytical
 * claims instead (positioning §4).
 *
 * GITHUB HAS LEFT THE GLOBAL SHELL (Huayin, 2026-08-27, cold-reader cleanup item 2). It used to sit
 * here as a "utility action" — secondary to the doors, but present on every page of the site. In the
 * current reader architecture that is a category error: the repository is COLUMNA's source, not
 * datumwise's, and a global GitHub link makes the whole project read as one open-source repo with an
 * essay site attached. datumwise's reader-facing terrain is Foundations · Analytical Governance ·
 * Doors · Ask · Columna, and GitHub is not a peer of those. It is now reached where it means
 * something: /columna carries the source link in its own meta line, and the Columna footer variant
 * carries it on every Columna surface (see components/site/SiteFooter.astro). Do not restore it to
 * the shell as a "small" utility — small is how it got here the first time.
 *
 * Navigation hierarchy does not require URL hierarchy (positioning §10), so these hrefs are the
 * canonical destinations, not a folder tree.
 *
 * NO-JS: the mobile menu is a <details> disclosure. It opens, closes, and is keyboard-operable
 * with JavaScript disabled, and the same four doors are inside it — no nested information
 * architecture, nothing hidden behind an intellectual hierarchy.
 */
// FOUNDATIONS TAKES THE FIRST DOOR, RESEARCH LEAVES THE HEADER (Huayin, ruling R8, 2026-08-27).
//
// The ruled reader-facing architecture is Foundations · Analytical Governance · Doors · Ask ·
// Columna, and the same ruling says explicitly that it is NOT a requirement that all five become
// equal navigation items. Doors does not exist yet and Ask has no reviewed answers yet, so a literal
// five-item header would advertise two rooms that are not furnished. This is a SUBSTITUTION, not an
// expansion: the header stays at three flat doors and the count is unchanged.
//
// /research is not demoted, it is RE-PLACED. It remains the publication and source estate, it keeps
// its footer link on every page of the site, and it is now reached contextually from the surface
// that gives a reader a reason to want it — /foundations ends by sending them there, and every
// /read page opens with a link back to it. A reader arriving at the corpus from a foundational
// question is better served than one arriving at it from a nav bar.
// RENAMED `DOORS` → `NAV` (2026-08-27). The word "Doors" now names a reader-facing surface —
// /doors, the editorial passage collection — and this constant names the three primary navigation
// items, which are explicitly NOT peers of that. Two unrelated meanings of "doors" in one shell,
// one of them in code, is the ambiguity a one-word rename removes. The CSS class `.doors` on the
// <nav> is the navigation's own, unrelated to the route, and is left alone — and now that /doors IS
// one of the items, the rename is what keeps `NAV` legible rather than self-referential.
//
// DOORS ENTERS THE HEADER (Huayin, 2026-08-27, superseding the "not yet" clause of R8). The reason
// R8 held it back has EXPIRED, and the ruling is explicit about why: the count of entries never
// determined whether Doors is a legitimate destination. It is live, coherent and complete as a
// reader-facing function at three pieces; The Yes Machine Problem will make it four without
// changing what it is. So this is an EXPANSION to four, not the substitution R8 performed — the
// first deliberate widening of the shell, and the bar for the next one is the same as this one:
// a ruling that says the surface is a destination in its own right.
//
// PLACED THIRD, NOT LAST. The order is the reader's terrain in the order the ruling states it:
// Foundations · Analytical Governance · Doors · Columna. Doors sits after the two territories
// (what a reader should understand) and before Columna (what was built from it), which is where a
// browsing reader is actually caught.
//
// ASK IS STILL NOT HERE, deliberately (Huayin, same ruling): its navigation role is a question for
// the upcoming Ask mission, not a symmetry to be completed now. Do not add it to this array as
// tidying-up.
//
// ═══ THE SHELVES REPLACE THE MIXTURE (Huayin + CG2, 2026-08-30) ═══════════════════════════════════
//
// The rail above was Foundations · Analytical Governance · Doors · Columna: a LAYER, a single
// paper's DOORWAY, a reader-entry ROLE, and a SYSTEM. Four items, four different kinds of thing, and
// a reader learns the false parallel on first glance, before any page can correct it.
//
// It is now Start Here · Foundations · Architecture · Columna. Three shelves and one entrance.
//
// ARCHITECTURE IS A NEW SHELF, NOT A RENAME. `/analytical-governance` held a rail slot because there
// was nowhere else to put it — and it is the doorway to ONE deposited record, which it may compress
// and connect but explicitly not extend. It keeps its URL, its prose and its history route; it is
// now a work INSIDE /architecture rather than a peer of Foundations. That is a correction of level,
// not a demotion: its prominence is preserved by /architecture presenting three major works with
// their governing questions, which is more coherent than an unexplained nav item.
//
// DOORS LEAVES THE RAIL, REVERSING AN EXPLICIT RULING — SO THE REASON IS RECORDED. It entered on
// 2026-08-27 as "the first deliberate widening of the shell", on the ground that the count of
// entries never determined whether Doors is a legitimate destination. That reasoning is not
// withdrawn and /doors is not demoted: it stays live, unchanged, and is surfaced from the homepage
// and from Start Here, where a first-time reader actually is.
//
// What changed is the question the rail answers. A Door is a ROLE — what a piece does for a reader —
// and pages/doors.astro rules exactly that: "DOOR IS A ROLE, NOT AN ARTIFACT TYPE. It is not a
// publication type, an authority class, a provenance class, a standing, or a corpus layer." The
// other three items answer "what kind of work is this?". A rail cannot ask both questions at once
// without teaching the reader that Doors is a fourth kind of work. It is not; a Foundations or
// Architecture paper may ALSO be a Door.
//
// START HERE TAKES THE FIRST SLOT, AND MEETS THE 2026-08-27 BAR. That ruling set the price of the
// next widening: "a ruling that says the surface is a destination in its own right." /start-here is
// one — it renders a complete artifact and is where a new reader is meant to land. It is also not a
// shelf, and it does not pretend to be: it is the entrance, placed first because that is what an
// entrance is.
//
// CROSS-LISTING IS EXPECTED, NOT A DEFECT (Huayin, 2026-08-30). A work may sit on two shelves
// because a reader might look for it on either. Do NOT build a membership rule that forces a choice
// between Foundations and Architecture; the shelves exist to help people find things, and the
// practical task is sharing the work, not maintaining a taxonomy.
//
// ASK IS STILL NOT HERE, deliberately, and for the same reason as before. Do not add it as
// tidying-up.
const NAV = [
  { label: 'Start Here', href: '/start-here' },
  { label: 'Foundations', href: '/foundations' },
  { label: 'Architecture', href: '/architecture' },
  { label: 'Columna', href: '/columna' },
];
const here = Astro.url.pathname.replace(/\/$/, '') || '/';
const isCurrent = (href: string) => here === href || here.startsWith(href + '/');
---

<header class="shell ds ds-paper">
  <a href="/" class="wordmark">datumwise</a>

  <nav class="doors" aria-label="Primary">
    {NAV.map((d) => (
      <a href={d.href} aria-current={isCurrent(d.href) ? 'page' : undefined}>{d.label}</a>
    ))}
  </nav>

  <details class="menu">
    <summary aria-label="Menu"><span aria-hidden="true">Menu</span></summary>
    <nav class="sheet" aria-label="Primary, compact">
      {NAV.map((d) => (
        <a href={d.href} aria-current={isCurrent(d.href) ? 'page' : undefined}>{d.label}</a>
      ))}
    </nav>
  </details>
</header>

<style>
  .shell {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--ds-space-4);
    padding: 1.1rem var(--ds-gutter);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ds-rule);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }
  .wordmark {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  .doors { display: flex; gap: var(--ds-space-3); }
  .doors a {
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding-bottom: 3px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .doors a:hover { color: var(--ink); border-bottom-color: var(--ds-rule-strong); }
  /* the current door is marked by rule + ink, not by colour alone */
  .doors a[aria-current='page'] { color: var(--ink); border-bottom-color: var(--ink); }

  .menu { display: none; }
  .menu summary {
    list-style: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ds-rule-strong);
    padding-bottom: 2px;
  }
  .menu summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .menu[open] summary { color: var(--ink); }
  .sheet { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; padding: 1.1rem 0 0.25rem; }
  .sheet a {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ds-rule);
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* Compact shell. The same three doors move into the sheet — nothing is demoted or nested. */
  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .shell { flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--ds-space-2); }
    .doors { display: none; }
    .menu { display: block; }
    .menu[open] { width: 100%; }
  }
</style>
